                            {
                                "id": "communicate",
                                "title": "1. Communicating / Transferring Results",
                                "image": "assets/methods/covers/hines-c6-communicate.svg",
                                "imageCaption": "Audience style, immersion, modular outputs",
                                "slideTitles": [
                                    "Definition",
                                    "Hines guidelines",
                                    "Why it matters"
                                ],
                                "paragraphs": [
                                    "<p>Communicating results designs foresight for transfer into the organization's decision routines.<br><br>Move abstract futures into forms decision makers can use in their thinking style. Immerse stakeholders carefully. Modularize outputs. Transferability beats archive beauty.<br><br>So what? Provocation is calibrated to culture, not performed for effect.</p>",
                                    "<p>In practice, Hines and Bishop point the analyst to:</p><ul class=\"fitness-pres-bullets\"><li>Designing results for transferability</li><li>Arranging the message in the audience's thinking style</li><li>Immersing stakeholders and decision makers in alternatives for acceptance and alignment</li><li>Being provocative (with care; fit organizational culture)</li><li>Modularizing outputs: keep the good, address the bad</li><li>Awareness of change through experience, insight, and reframing</li></ul>",
                                    "<p>Untransferred foresight is unfinished work.<br><br>Immersion raises uptake. Modular outputs let decision makers keep what helps and challenge what hurts. Lived reframe is how change awareness sticks.<br><br>So what? Write for the decision maker's language, not for the archive.<br><br><strong>Applied example:</strong> A CFO will not read a 40-page scenario narrative. A modular one-page options brief with cost gates and decision dates transfers the same foresight into budget language.</p>"
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "id": "agenda",
                                "title": "2. Action Agenda",
                                "image": "assets/methods/covers/hines-c6-agenda.svg",
                                "imageCaption": "Urgency, milestones, one improbable bet",
                                "slideTitles": [
                                    "Definition",
                                    "Hines guidelines",
                                    "Why it matters"
                                ],
                                "paragraphs": [
                                    "<p>An action agenda turns Planning into near-term moves the organization will actually schedule: urgency, better decisions, milestones, and at least one improbable idea worth a controlled bet.<br><br>Build from strengths. Decide without perfect data. Celebrate small wins toward the preferred future.<br><br>So what? The point is better decisions, not perfect foresight.</p>",
                                    "<p>In practice, Hines and Bishop point the analyst to:</p><ul class=\"fitness-pres-bullets\"><li>Creating a sense of urgency</li><li>Strengthening what the organization already does well and building from there</li><li>Purpose of activity: help better decisions</li><li>Decide without all desired data (avoid analysis paralysis)</li><li>Milestones toward the preferred future and celebrating small wins</li><li>Recommend investing at least in one improbable idea</li></ul>",
                                    "<p>Urgency without capability collapses.<br><br>Strength-based moves and milestones make progress visible. One improbable investment keeps the portfolio from only safe incrementalism.<br><br>So what? Schedule moves people can start, including one controlled stretch bet.<br><br><strong>Applied example:</strong> Besides process upgrades, the agenda funds one improbable pilot: stackable micro-credentials co-designed with employers, with a 90-day milestone and a kill or scale gate.</p>"
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "id": "sis",
                                "title": "3. Strategic Information System",
                                "image": "assets/methods/covers/hines-c6-sis.svg",
                                "imageCaption": "Early warning linked to planning",
                                "slideTitles": [
                                    "Definition",
                                    "Hines guidelines",
                                    "Why it matters"
                                ],
                                "paragraphs": [
                                    "<p>A strategic information system keeps Acting alive after the workshop: foresight-aligned indicators linked to planning gates, with early warning for weak signals that would reopen Framing or Forecasting.<br><br>Seek turbulence sources and crisis indicators that are few and easy to collect. SIS must feed decisions, not fill a data lake.<br><br>So what? Unwatched indicators are decoration.</p>",
                                    "<p>In practice, Hines and Bishop point the analyst to:</p><ul class=\"fitness-pres-bullets\"><li>An information system aligned with foresight and linked to planning</li><li>An early warning system for weak signals</li><li>Searching for sources of turbulence in the system</li><li>Indicators that signal a potential crisis</li><li>Indicators that are easy to understand and collect</li></ul>",
                                    "<p>Unwatched indicators are decoration.<br><br>Weak signals and turbulence extend Scanning into operations. Few easy indicators beat a dashboard nobody owns.<br><br>So what? Watch a short list that can reopen planning, not a vanity dashboard.<br><br><strong>Applied example:</strong> A university watches three early-warning indicators monthly: application yield by program, employer skill requests shifting toward AI fluency, and policy drafts on generative tools. Each triggers a planning review, not a dashboard vanity metric.</p>"
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "id": "institutionalize",
                                "title": "4. Institutionalizing Strategic Thinking",
                                "image": "assets/methods/covers/hines-c6-institutionalize.svg",
                                "imageCaption": "Framework, rhythm, learning organization",
                                "slideTitles": [
                                    "Definition",
                                    "Hines guidelines",
                                    "Why it matters"
                                ],
                                "paragraphs": [
                                    "<p>Institutionalizing strategic thinking makes foresight a recurring capability after the first project: clear frame, futures rhythm, training, and a learning organization that can accept change.<br><br>Make the conceptual framework explicit. Repeat strategic foresight activities on a cadence.<br><br>So what? One-off success that freezes is still failure of Acting.</p>",
                                    "<p>In practice, Hines and Bishop point the analyst to:</p><ul class=\"fitness-pres-bullets\"><li>Selecting, designing, and making the conceptual framework explicit</li><li>Developing a futures rhythm (regular cadence of study and attention)</li><li>Regular repetition of strategic foresight activities</li><li>Training programs for institutionalization</li><li>In a complex environment, learning is the best approach (learning organization)</li><li>Shifting attitudes toward accepting change</li></ul>",
                                    "<p>An explicit frame stops every project reinventing method language.<br><br>Rhythm and training beat one-off reports. Change acceptance keeps Acting from freezing after first success.<br><br>So what? Build a calendar and a shared language, not only a successful pilot.<br><br><strong>Applied example:</strong> Instead of one annual workshop, the organization runs a quarterly foresight hour tied to budget cycles, with a short internal training so new managers inherit the six-pillar language.</p>"
                                ]
                            }
